#+TITLE: cadgui-6-3d-gui — 3D GUI (BricsCAD-like)
#+STATUS: DEFERRED
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: cadgui-5-3d-file
#+TARGET: release 3.0
#+BRANCH: qt6-gui
#+DATE: 2026-11-16
#+PARENT: cadgui-release-3.0-epic

* Context

Phase 6 of [[cadgui-release-3.0-epic]], the last: the interactive 3D modelling
UI, BricsCAD-like. Demand-gated; begins only after 3D file support
([[cadgui-5-3d-file]]) lands and on customer need.

* Pre-work

Depends on the 3D-UX verification carried in [[cadgui-5-3d-file]] (how
AutoCAD/BricsCAD actually do 3D modelling UI, confirmed against vendor docs):
that decides whether to match BricsCAD's direct-modelling model, AutoCAD's, or
pick one coherent UX. Settle before scoping the commands here.

* Deliverables

- Direct-modelling commands (=DM*=-style: push/pull, move/rotate faces, fillet).
- The 3D manipulator / gizmo + a quad-like context HUD.
- Orbit / navigation (viewcube-style), camera + view management.
- Subobject (face/edge/vertex) selection + editing.
- Visual styles (wireframe / hidden / shaded / realistic).
- Optional: parametric 3D constraints (matching BricsCAD here is a large add;
  gate separately).

* Dependencies / sequencing

After [[cadgui-5-3d-file]]. The GPU display path (OpenGL/Qt3D vs the 2D QPainter
canvas) is decided here, reusing phase-2's display-list boundary extended to 3D.
UX "feel" (the manipulator, orbit) is the human-judgement limiter, as the 2D
canvas feel was in phase 2.

* Test & doc approach

- Direct-modelling ops: headless Action-Layer tests (invoke push/pull, assert the
  resulting B-rep) + offscreen renders of the result.
- Navigation/manipulator: scripted-interaction tests via the Action Layer.
- Doc: a 3D-GUI section of the cadgui spec + user-manual.

* Acceptance themes

- [ ] Direct-modelling commands create/edit solids, driven headless + from the UI.
- [ ] Orbit/navigation + the manipulator work interactively.
- [ ] Subobject selection + editing.
- [ ] Visual styles render (offscreen-capable).
- [ ] Every 3D interaction registered + headless-tested through the Action Layer.
